export default "import { Meta } from '@storybook/addon-docs/blocks';\n\n<Meta title=\"Docs/Architecture Vision\" />\n\n# The Dynamic Semantic Canvas\n\n> *One surface. Any payload. Any user. The interface never changes.*\n\n---\n\n## What Is Being Built\n\nWe built a **Dynamic Semantic Canvas** — a single, stable interface surface that uses AI as a runtime compiler to serve infinite operational workflows. One surface. Any payload. Any user.\n\nA field technician types in a hardware model and watches the canvas generate a wiring schematic. A compliance officer queries maritime chemical storage regulations and gets chapter-and-verse from the American Bureau of Shipping. A product manager inputs raw business requirements, selects a Figma design system token set, and watches the AI assemble a production-grade interface from pre-approved atomic components. A UX designer monitors performance telemetry on the same surface, in the same session, without ever leaving the environment.\n\nThe interface never changes. The AI delivers different levels of access, different rendering modes, different output formats — but the human experience remains **cognitively stable**. That's the architecture.\n\n### The Problem We're Solving\n\n**The Silo Wall.** For two decades, software has been trapped in a structural handoff pipeline. A designer creates a static mockup. It crosses an invisible silo wall into engineering. At that moment, the design intent — the accessibility reasoning, the interaction logic, the user context — becomes optional. It becomes subject to interpretation by people who were never in the room when the design decisions were made.\n\nThis isn't anyone's fault. It's a **sociological artifact** of how corporations organized labor during the early web era. C. Wright Mills called this the sociological imagination — the ability to see individual problems as structural ones. The handoff wall is not a personal failure of any engineer or product manager. It's the inevitable output of a system that treats design as an assembly line.\n\n**The AI Discourse Problem.** The industry is obsessed with AI domination narratives — autonomous agents, superintelligence, machines replacing humans. This is a profound category error. An all-powerful intelligence with no human interface, no human utility, and no human observer is like a God with no one to perceive Him. It's meaningless. Intelligence without a surface for human perception is compute running in a dark room.\n\nThe real frontier isn't AI autonomy. It's **A2UI** — Agent-to-User Interfaces. It's the question of how we build interfaces that can dynamically adapt to AI-generated content without sacrificing brand integrity, layout stability, or design system compliance.\n\n---\n\n## Features\n\n### Grounding Metrics — Trust Is Not Abstract\n\nFive metrics run against every AI output: faithfulness, hallucination, recall, relevance, completeness. That sounds clinical. Here's what it actually means:\n\nYour monthly grocery order went through the AI. Gnocchi was substituted for dumplings. The grounding trace shows exactly where the substitution happened — the inventory API returned a null, the fallback logic triggered, the model chose the closest match. You click the flagged moment, add a constraint: \"Never substitute gnocchi.\" Rerun. Fixed.\n\nGrounding metrics aren't about model performance. They're about **your money, your dinner, your trust**. When an AI manages your checking account and something goes wrong, you need to walk backwards through every decision until you find the butterfly wing that caused the storm.\n\n### Safety Constraints — Guardrails Are Personal\n\nThe Constraints section in every prompt isn't a corporate checkbox. It's where you say: \"Never order more than my budget.\" \"Don't cancel my insurance.\" \"Flag any transaction over $500.\" These aren't system rules — they're **your rules**. The system enforces them not because compliance requires it, but because you shouldn't have to think about them every time.\n\n### The Trace — The Butterfly's Wing\n\nEvery API call. Every model decision. Every substitution. When your balance is off, when your order is wrong, when something happened that you didn't intend — you open the Trace tab and walk backwards through time until you find the moment. Not a log file. A story.\n\nThis isn't debugging. It's **accountability without blame**. The gap between what you meant and what the system produced — that's what the trace closes.\n\n### Versioning — Prompts Get Smarter Over Time\n\nEvery RUN snapshots to Milvus. Every save creates a PostgreSQL version. Open a prompt from last month and you see its entire history — what worked, what failed, what you changed. The prompt isn't disposable. It's an **asset that compounds**. Your H-E-B order prompt from January had 3 errors. By July it has none. You didn't get better at prompting — the prompt got better through use.\n\n### The Canvas — The Interface Disappears\n\nThe three columns aren't the product. They're **training wheels**. Collapse the left column — the prompt is hidden, you're just watching the form fill itself. Collapse the right column — the assistant steps back, you're in full-screen output. Collapse both — it's just you and the work. A schematic on a tablet. A document on a monitor. A chat on a phone.\n\nThe interface fades. What's left is the human doing what humans do. The AI assists, then recedes. That's not a feature. That's the **summum bonum** of software design.\n\n### The Prompt Repository — Everyone Needs a Place\n\nDevelopers have code prompts. Designers have Figma prompts. Executives have decision prompts. Field techs have diagnostic prompts. Right now those live in scattered notes, chat histories, and memory. The prompt repository is the missing primitive — **a place to keep your AI stuff managed**.\n\n### Prompt Layers — Why Sections Beat a Text Box\n\nA single text box is a monologue. Six named, swappable sections are an assembly line. Here's what structured layers unlock.\n\n**Different ownership.** Different teams own different layers. UX owns the System Role — the AI's identity and expertise. Product owns the Constraints — the hard rules. Engineering owns the Tool Call — the APIs and data sources. Legal owns the Context — the regulatory background. Nobody steps on anyone else's work. When the gnocchi substitution happens, the trace shows exactly which team's layer caused it.\n\n**Portability.** Layers aren't trapped inside prompts — they're a library. Open any prompt, browse \"Existing User Roles\" across the entire enterprise, pick one. Sections become searchable, reusable assets. You compose prompts by assembling pre-built, battle-tested layers, not by typing from scratch. This is also where **data dignity** enters — your most effective Constraint block, your perfect System Role, your optimized Tool Call — these are intellectual property. The marketplace doesn't sell prompts. It sells layers.\n\n**Layer switching by audience.** Same core prompt, different System Role. Compliance sees the full regulatory layer. Marketing sees the brand-safe output layer. Engineering sees the technical spec layer. One prompt, three views, zero duplication.\n\n**Layer sequencing.** Run Constraints layer first — \"Does this output violate anything?\" If clean, pass to Few Shot layer — \"Match these exemplar tones.\" If not, loop back. Each layer is a gate. The trace shows which layer passed and which failed.\n\n**Layer inheritance.** Create a base prompt with System Role + Constraints. Fork it into 50 department-specific prompts that inherit the base. Update the base Constraints once — all 50 children update. Like CSS cascading, but for AI instructions.\n\n**Section-level provenance.** Every output carries a genealogy: \"System Role from Compliance Team, User Role from UX Research, Constraints from Legal, Tool Call from Engineering.\" When an output outperforms, you know which combination of layers produced the win. When it fails, you know which layer to fix.\n\n---\n\n## The Architecture\n\n### One Surface, Infinite Workflows\n\nThe middle output area is a universal rendering surface. It can generate:\n\n- **Vector schematics** — replacing a GE solar panel with a Vektron model in a field wiring diagram\n- **Regulatory documents** — querying a knowledge base for ABS maritime specifications\n- **Word processing documents** — CEO-ready presentations generated from raw meeting transcripts\n- **Design system components** — live React components assembled from Figma-synced atomic tokens\n- **Audio/visual routing layouts** — matrix switcher grids for live event production\n- **Anything a user can describe in a prompt** — because the surface doesn't care what it renders\n\nThe UI remains constant. A left column for prompt composition, a center column for output, a right column for versioning and telemetry. The cognitive load on the user never changes, regardless of the complexity of the underlying AI operation.\n\n### Atomic Design as Mathematical Guardrails\n\nThe AI cannot generate arbitrary interfaces. It is structurally constrained by an atomic design system — Brad Frost's atoms, molecules, and organisms mapped to a live Figma token library. When a product manager selects \"Design System #5 for Insurance,\" the AI inherits a hard boundary. Every generated layout, every new component combination, every experimental schematic is **natively bound to that corporate vertical from its first rendered pixel**.\n\nIf an existing molecule exists (a specialized input field, a compliance badge), the AI must use it. If the user demands a new concept, the AI synthesizes a new molecule from the pre-approved atoms already living in the Figma-synchronized library. This is **controlled evolution** — innovation within guardrails.\n\n### UX as Performance Governance\n\nUX designers stop being wireframe factories and become **Guardrail Architects**. The traditional meeting is replaced by asynchronous governance: product teams ideate autonomously, push to UX with one click, and UX optimizes performance — adding deliberate friction on high-risk operations, verifying accessibility, mapping telemetry hooks. The code that leaves the canvas is already structured, tokenized, and verified. The entire lifecycle happens **without a single meeting**.\n\n### Telemetry as Accountability\n\nWe don't just claim responsibility for failures — we engineer it into the infrastructure. API call tracking, rendering crash capture, breadcrumb trails, a debug panel exposing live logs and session metadata. When the AI generates a broken schematic or a database query times out, it's not a mystery buried in server logs. It's a Sentry event with full context and a graceful fallback that keeps the human in control.\n\n---\n\n## Three-Column Agentic IDE\n\n```\n┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│  HEADER: Console | Composer | Components | Eval | Vars       │\n├──────────────┬───────────────────────┬───────────────────────┤\n│  LEFT        │  THIRD COLUMN         │  RIGHT                │\n│  Composer    │  Output Workspace     │  Chat + Trace         │\n│              │                       │                       │\n│  System Role │  [Model: DeepSeek ▼]  │  AI Assistant         │\n│  User Role   │  ┌─────────────────┐  │  (surface controller) │\n│  Tool Call   │  │                 │  │                       │\n│  Few Shot    │  │  Live Output    │  │  Tab: Chat            │\n│  Context     │  │  (editable,     │  │  Tab: Trace           │\n│  Constraints │  │   streamable)   │  │  Tab: Variables       │\n│  + Custom    │  │                 │  │                       │\n│              │  └─────────────────┘  │  Grounding metrics    │\n│  Drag-drop   │  [Copy] [Save] [Exp]  │  Version history      │\n│  Collapse    │  [Regenerate] [Clear] │  Audit trail          │\n│  Attachments │                       │                       │\n│              │  Beforeunload guard   │                       │\n│  [Save] [RUN]│  Locked when active   │                       │\n└──────────────┴───────────────────────┴───────────────────────┘\n```\n\n| Column | Role | Components |\n|--------|------|------------|\n| **Left** | Agentic Flow Builder | System Role, User Role, Tool Call, Few Shot, Context, Constraints, Custom Roles |\n| **Third** | Output Workspace | Streaming output, model selector, editable text, save/export/regenerate |\n| **Right** | AI Assistant + Audit | Session-scoped Chat, Milvus Trace, Variable detection |\n\n---\n\n## Technical Foundation\n\n| Layer | Technology |\n|-------|-----------|\n| Frontend | React 19, Vite 7, TypeScript, Tailwind CSS 4, shadcn/ui |\n| Backend | FastAPI (Python 3.14), Uvicorn |\n| Database | PostgreSQL + Milvus Lite |\n| Design-to-Code | Figma Make → custom transform pipeline |\n| Monitoring | Sentry (error boundaries, API tracking, performance) |\n| Deployment | GitHub Actions CI/CD → SiteGround |\n\n---\n\n*This is the architecture of the Raibach Design System Lifecycle Management platform — a universal prompt surface where humans give direction, AI executes, and the interface disappears.*\n"